{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793473480,"nonce":"RvmQAYZwn452KcMukm3e59zyMmQ=","title":"Python: string arguments passed through call_raw arrive empty","message":"Reported by the dynASB embedding session (their git-bug 5b7d11d), at 7a709d93 on x86_64 Linux. `rt.call_raw(\"handle\", [ZyntaxValue::from(\"in\"), ZyntaxValue::from(\"m1\"), ZyntaxValue::Int(10)])` against `def handle(port: str, data: str, n: int) -\u003e int` returns the right Int, but `port + \":\" + data` inside the handler is \"::\". Int arguments arrive intact. Either call_raw does not marshal a ZyntaxValue string to a `str` parameter, or it should refuse one; the workaround is a host getter returning NativeType::Str (as tests/host_native.rs does).","files":null}]}